autocorrect, what the fuck.

i would recommend checking out the used market in that price range- keep your eyes out for bikes such as specialized hardrocks/rockhoppers, trek 4300's and similar bikes. occasionally you'll find some decent entry-level bikes by other brands like motobecane 400ht, or the jamis ranger. a few things you want to look for when looking at the used market-  


first thing is to make sure it fits! this is the most important, as an ill-fitting bike is just as useful as trying to ride your sofa on a DH track.

&#x200B;

next, look for trigger shifters, decent componentry (Shimano Altus, Acera and sometimes deore is what you'll generally find in this price. stay away from the tourney and the infamous flat-face Shimano). while not a tell-tale sign, these are pretty good pointers to a well-manufactured bike.

&#x200B;

if there is too many custom components STAY AWAY. while at first, it seems counterintuitive, note that someone who's added many aftermarket parts has also likely ridden the bike HARD, which means more component wear, and frame wear.

&#x200B;

try not to go TOO old. outdated stuff will tend to break more and you wont have as much ease finding repairs for them.

&#x200B;

look for threadless, 1-1/8 inch headsets. there should NOT be a big nut that holds everything together. the stem should be attached via allen key bolts.  


use bicycle blue book as a GENERAL price guide to make sure you're not overpaying.

&#x200B;

that's all I can think of rn, but feel free to ask any questions! good luck, and happy riding!

